{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 7371a9b86c693a443fc27281eda6230477e7e4c8, against round-9 report e73c863f01bc1723ddeefe98336d63a29b536c89. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nThe blocker was mine and it was introduced by the previous round's fix, which is now the third time that has happened in this file. Saying an all-refused batch has no E contradicted the claim record and the reconciliation table at once: the claim records P, M and E, and the P/P/live row tells a recovering run to publish the recorded E, so a crash after acquiring an all-refused claim landed on a row it could not perform. Your first option is the right one and I took it. E = P for that batch. Every existing row stays correct, no second transaction needs specifying, and publishing E = P is a no-op fast-forward to where the sequence already is — which is exactly the correct action when nothing was admitted. The ledger commit still takes only the previous ledger head as parent, since E = P is already reachable and there is nothing new to retain.\n\nI also put it at the definition site rather than leaving it to be discovered several sections later: the prebuilt chain may be empty, so E = P, and E is always defined for a drain holding a claim. The original definition already implied this — chaining zero admitted events onto P yields P — so the earlier \"no E\" sentence was not just under-specified, it contradicted the definition it was supposedly elaborating.\n\nPayload is now concrete: one line per submission in the ledger commit's message body, identity plus result plus either the event commit or the refusal reason, every considered submission appearing exactly once. Message body rather than a tree, so the submitter's search needs no tree walk, and the shape matches what your probe already recovered. The every-submission-exactly-once property is what lets an auditor reconcile a batch against the sequence instead of trusting the drain's summary.\n\nSpike case 4 gains the executable form. Run with those two submissions as the only ones, it *is* an all-refused batch, so it now asserts the claim records E = P, the ledger entry has exactly one parent, and a kill after claim acquisition reconciles through the P/P/live row. I would rather this rule be checked by execution than stated well, given it is a rule I got wrong by assertion one round ago.","body":{"commit":"7371a9b86c693a443fc27281eda6230477e7e4c8","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}